# /etc/apivo/<env>/blnk-migrate.env — 0600 root:root
#
# The MIGRATION half of the ledger's configuration: what the one-shot
# `blnk migrate up` container reads, for the few seconds it exists on each
# rollout. The runtime half is blnk.env.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why this is a separate FILE and not a second key in blnk.env
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Blnk reads its DSN from `BLNK_DATA_SOURCE_DNS` whichever job it is doing —
# migrating or serving. So the only way to hand each container a different
# value through `env_file`, and keep both out of the reconciler's environment
# and out of `docker compose config` output, is one file per role.
#
# The alternative — both DSNs in blnk.env, with the migrate container
# overriding one — would put the OWNER's credential in the environment of the
# long-lived server process. A compromised ledger could then read it out of
# its own environment and connect as the owner, which is exactly the
# capability the split exists to remove. Two files means the owner credential
# is ABSENT from the continuous processes, not merely unused by them.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BLNK_DATA_SOURCE_DNS — the DATABASE OWNER, not blnk_app.
#
# The same role `DATABASE_URL` in api.env uses. Migrations are DDL: Blnk's
# first migration issues `CREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS blnk`, and PostgreSQL
# checks the database-level CREATE privilege BEFORE it takes the IF NOT EXISTS
# shortcut — so pre-creating the schema does not avoid the grant. A single
# role doing both jobs would need CREATE on the database permanently, for one
# statement on one deploy. The founder took the split instead (2026-08-24), so
# the role exposed continuously is the narrow one.
#
#   qa       postgres://apivo:<APIVO_PG_PASSWORD>@postgres:5432/apivo?sslmode=require
#   staging  postgres://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:5432/postgres?sslmode=verify-full
#   prod
#
# This value is usually IDENTICAL to DATABASE_URL in api.env, and it is still
# written twice on purpose: the api and the ledger are separate consumers, and
# a deployment that later gives the ledger its own owning role should not have
# to disentangle one value serving two meanings.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
BLNK_DATA_SOURCE_DNS=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BLNK_SERVER_SECRET_KEY is deliberately ABSENT.
#
# It configures the SERVER's authentication, and this container never serves
# anything — it migrates and exits. Blnk validates its whole configuration
# before running any command, which is why BLNK_REDIS_DNS is set in the
# compose file for a job that never touches Redis; nothing else is needed.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
